909 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 228 0 R
/T (Correspondence analysis is an exploratory statistical method used to examine the co-occurrence of categories of nominal variables. Its main purpose is to provide a graphical representation of relationships and associations between categories of two or more qualitative variables. This makes it easier to interpret the data and identify underlying structures. The technique allows nominal data to be presented using one-, two-, or three-dimensional plots, which are used to visualize relationships between the categories of the variables being analyzed.)
/E (Correspondence analysis is an exploratory statistical method used to examine the co-occurrence of categories of nominal variables. Its main purpose is to provide a graphical representation of relationships and associations between categories of two or more qualitative variables. This makes it easier to interpret the data and identify underlying structures. The technique allows nominal data to be presented using one-, two-, or three-dimensional plots, which are used to visualize relationships between the categories of the variables being analyzed.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
910 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (Correspondence analysis has strong theoretical foundations derived from research on the chi-square statistic. At the beginning of the 20th century, Pearson and Fisher conducted research on model fitting and the independence of variables in frequency tables. Pearson also introduced the mean-square contingency measure as a way of assessing the strength of relationships between qualitative variables. Further important developments in correspondence analysis took place in the 1930s and 1940s, particularly through the work of Hirschfeld, Fisher, and Guttman, although at that time the focus was primarily numerical rather than graphical.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
